## Codex Execution Order

Run them in this order:

```text
01  Repository Audit
 ↓
02  Extension Foundation
 ↓
03  Snippet Organization
 ↓
04  Authoring Toolkit
 ↓
05  Uriel Mythic Toolkit
 ↓
06  Documentation
 ↓
07  Automated Validation
 ↓
08  VSIX Packaging
 ↓
09  GitHub Hygiene
 ↓
10  Marketplace Preparation
 ↓
11  Final Release Audit
```

The important distinction is that **Codex should be allowed to inspect and test between stages**, rather than receiving one giant prompt to build everything blindly. This also gives a clean point at which to stop if the existing `uriel-studio` repository turns out to contain conflicting work.
